    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The handler responds `409 CONFLICT` and is already wired. The implementation is complete and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `householdId` is now deterministic \u2014 the first 12 hex chars of SHA-256 over `(normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode)` \u2014 so owners with the same last name and postcode share it automatically.\n\nChanges, kept as small cohesive units to minimize structural impact:\n\n1. **`HouseholdId.java`** (new, small helper \u2014 mirrors the existing `IdentityKey`/`CheckDigit` style) \u2014 computes the deterministic id from `(lastName, postcode)`, with an overload taking the raw strings so the request path can compute it without an `Owner`.\n\n2. **`Owner.getHouseholdId()`** \u2014 reduced to a one-line delegation to `HouseholdId.of(this)`, so the derivation moves out of the large entity class (which also flows into `identityKey`, since that reads `getHouseholdId()`).\n\n3. **`EnsureUniqueHousehold`** \u2014 now keys the duplicate block on the computed `householdId` (last name + postcode) instead of last name + address; `sharesHousehold` still bypasses it as a declared member. Wired into `owners.POST.yml` after the identity check, so a second same-household owner gets a **409** unless it opts in.\n\n4. **`PossibleDuplicate`** \u2014 added one guard so a same-household owner (equal `householdId`) is never flagged: a declared member is not a suspected duplicate.\n\n`./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the new `coreSameLastNameAndPostcodeShareHouseholdId` acceptance test passes), BUILD SUCCESS.\n\nOne note on `MembershipLevel`: the spec mentions \"the household-size input to the membership level,\" but the current `MembershipLevel` derives from email presence and namesake count \u2014 it has no household-size input to redirect \u2014 so nothing there needed changing. Everything that actually keys off the household (`identityKey`, the duplicate block, and `possibleDuplicate`) now uses the computed `householdId`.",
